 a tumultuous  relationship  hazel calling her a liar - Olivia when  poppy tries to get her to kill her  children poppy had three children with  William all three of which is heavily  implied she killed in her conversation  with Olivia she recounts how all of them  died notably her son who lost feeling in  his legs and died cold in her arms we  even get a glimpse of him in episode 6  he's the wheelchair boy Olivia sees in  her dream during the storm  poppy went mad believing she can save  her kids from the hardships of the world  by killing them or as she refers to it  as awakening them she then tries to get  Olivia to do the same with her children  and this mental episode could have been  partly brought upon by her  husband's infidelity William Hill aka  the tall man with the cane was Poppy's  husband we learned in Episode seven that  he walled himself in Hill House in an  attempt to escape from the demons fear  and guilt the fear and guilt coming from  his affair with a housemaid Horace  Dudley tells Hugh that he was born in  Hill House and his mother worked there  he could often hear his mother giggling  to herself outside their cottage as if  talking to someone leading me to believe  that horse is the illegitimate son of  William Hill and poppy likely knew about  her husband's love for women in Episode  nine she says to Olivia in episode 10  trish tells Theo that she once knew a  man who had two sisters fear and guilt  every day they turned him a little more  mad he walled himself in to get away  from them his fear and guilt made him  feel small but when he awoke he was tall  that was his dream that is the story of  William Hill we then have Abigail the  daughter of Horace and Clara Dudley we  first see her as Luke's imaginary friend  but at this point it's reasonable to  believe she is real it's only when  Olivia arrives back and has the poisoned  tea party that Abigail dies and joins  the countless other ghosts in Hill House  Horace and Clara seeing their daughter's  ghost make a deal with Hugh that they'll  keep his secret to save his children's  memory of Olivia as long as he doesn't  burn down Hill House this deal made out  of the love for their child who they can  now visit anytime they want  in the final episode Horace brings a  dying Clara to Hell House so she too can  be with Abigail forever we then have who  I like to call the Scotch drinking man  Ryan Quayle who is first seen in episode  2 and not again until episode 6 seeing  as this show defines a ghost broadly for  example they can be a memory daydream  grief or guilt among other things  Ryan is Shirley's guilty ghost she had a  one-night stand with him while out the  embalming convention very romantic and  she never told her husband about it he  continually haunts her as a reminder of  her guilt we also have Luke who for a  brief moment appears as a ghost to Nelly  his ghostly appearance is because he  actually dies for a brief moment before  being resuscitated we then have a cadre  of minor ghosts who appear as daydreams  hallucinate  fears and wishes there's mr. smiley the  manifestation of young Kelsie's  molesting foster father who took the  form of the floorboards witch Theo saw  the clock repairman whose sole purpose  in the plot is to prove to Steve that he  was seeing ghosts all the time but he  just didn't realize it we then have lead  Joey and Shirley who all turned into  manifestations of fears for Steven Luke  and Shirley herself the ghosts in  haunting of Hill House provide the  impetus for change in our characters  they are what make our characters  confront their traumas and it's the  ending of Hill House that really  solidifies this message only by  confronting our troubles can we no  longer be haunted by the past and strive  to live better lives as Nellie's  therapist says in Episode five her real  problem is not her siblings it's not  confronting the pain of her past the  events of Hill House created traumas for  our characters each one coping with them  in different ways from Luke's addiction  Theo's meaningless sex and inability to  open up Nellie's depression Shirley the  control freak or Steve's unwillingness  to see the truth their ways of coping  like Luke's drugs for example don't get  at the core of what caused these traumas  in the first place they are merely  band-aids  it's only when each character is  confronted by their worst fear in the  Red Room that they must face it head-on  there is no place to hide here Steve  must confront his belief that if he had  a child it'll turn out diseased like his  mother Shirley must confront her  infidelity Luke his addiction which he  overcomes by choosing not to die and  Theo her inability to open up and let  others love her bottling up our trauma  keeping secrets only makes things worse  and those things can haunt us perhaps  the greatest encapsulation of this is  when Shirley confronts her dead body in  episode 10 the funeral manager tells her  on the outside she is fixed and pretty  but on the inside she is a horror our  greatest Horrors are not what's on the  outside they are buried within us when  Nellie releases them all from their  dreams they each have a chance to  apologize to her but she tells them she  knows they all love each other  unconditionally and that love is all  that matters  the rest is confetti interesting is her  speech about time stating that she used  to think it was linear but it's more  like rain moments falling around  this explains how she as a younger girl  saw herself as the bent neck lady the  Red Room according to Nelly is like the  stomach of the house putting on faces so  it can slowly digest them Hugh even  tells Steve that the house has called  the Mack because they're like an  unfinished meal a stomach breaks down  into pieces what we put into it  just like how the Red Room breaks down  the crane family when Hugh and Steven  drive to Hill House Hugh makes reference  to the clock repairman in the overalls  with the 